"Any session will switch to this profile automatically when your hostname, username, and current path match one of the rules specified in the adjacent table.\n\nA rule may specify a username, hostname, path, or job. It can also specify combinations of them.\n\nFor example:\n\n * `user@host:/path`\n * `user@`\n * `host`\n * `/path`\n * `&job`\n\nHostnames, paths, and jobs may use `*` wildcards. When a rule that had matched no longer matches (e.g., because the hostname changes), the profile switches back. You can prevent that by adding `!` at the end of the rule. For example, `user@example.com!`.\n\nSee the [documentation](https://iterm2.com/automatic-profile-switching.html) for all the details." = "Any session will switch to this profile automatically when your hostname, username, and current path match one of the rules specified in the adjacent table.\n\nA rule may specify a username, hostname, path, or job. It can also specify combinations of them.\n\nFor example:\n\n * `user@host:/path`\n * `user@`\n * `host`\n * `/path`\n * `&job`\n\nHostnames, paths, and jobs may use `*` wildcards. When a rule that had matched no longer matches (e.g., because the hostname changes), the profile switches back. You can prevent that by adding `!` at the end of the rule. For example, `user@example.com!`.\n\nSee the [documentation](https://iterm2.com/automatic-profile-switching.html) for all the details.";
"While this session has keyboard focus, the available Snippets will be limited to those containing all the tags listed in this field." = "While this session has keyboard focus, the available Snippets will be limited to those containing all the tags listed in this field.";
"When you double-click in the terminal, a single word is selected. When your click is on a letter, the word boundary is found by grouping letters adjacent to the click location. You can extend the set of characters that are treated as belonging to a word with this setting.\n\nFor example, if `/` is considered part of a word, then double clicking on a path would select all the path components.\n\nYou can choose to define the characters considered part of a word with a regular expression. Then you can add specific substrings, such as `https://`, if you would prefer double-clicks on URLs to include the scheme. Generally the regular expression you provide will be a sequence of alternates separated by `|`." = "When you double-click in the terminal, a single word is selected. When your click is on a letter, the word boundary is found by grouping letters adjacent to the click location. You can extend the set of characters that are treated as belonging to a word with this setting.\n\nFor example, if `/` is considered part of a word, then double clicking on a path would select all the path components.\n\nYou can choose to define the characters considered part of a word with a regular expression. Then you can add specific substrings, such as `https://`, if you would prefer double-clicks on URLs to include the scheme. Generally the regular expression you provide will be a sequence of alternates separated by `|`.";
"When enabled, modifiers are remapped in a way that affects system shortcuts and third-party apps that receive keystrokes in the background. For example, if you remap the  ⌥ to ⌘ and **Remap modifiers globally** is enabled then you could press ⌥-Tab to switch apps." = "When enabled, modifiers are remapped in a way that affects system shortcuts and third-party apps that receive keystrokes in the background. For example, if you remap the  ⌥ to ⌘ and **Remap modifiers globally** is enabled then you could press ⌥-Tab to switch apps.";
"Tabs: Darkness (in [0…1]) for non-selected tabs in non-Minimal theme in light mode." = "Tabs: Darkness (in [0…1]) for non-selected tabs in non-Minimal theme in light mode.";
"Tabs: Darkness (in [0…1]) for non-selected tabs in non-Minimal theme in dark mode." = "Tabs: Darkness (in [0…1]) for non-selected tabs in non-Minimal theme in dark mode.";
"Mouse: Scroll wheel up sends the specified text when in alternate screen mode.\nThe value should use Vim syntax, such as \\e for escape." = "Mouse: Scroll wheel up sends the specified text when in alternate screen mode.\nThe value should use Vim syntax, such as \\e for escape.";
"Mouse: Scroll wheel down sends the specified text when in alternate screen mode.\nThe value should use Vim syntax, such as \\e for escape." = "Mouse: Scroll wheel down sends the specified text when in alternate screen mode.\nThe value should use Vim syntax, such as \\e for escape.";
"Windows: DEPRECATED: Use “How should new instances be opened” instead.\nOpen files in split panes.\nThis affects shell scripts opened from Finder, for example. You must set “Open files in new windows, not new tabs” to “No” for this to take effect. See also “When opening a file in a split pane, should it be a vertical split?”" = "Windows: DEPRECATED: Use “How should new instances be opened” instead.\nOpen files in split panes.\nThis affects shell scripts opened from Finder, for example. You must set “Open files in new windows, not new tabs” to “No” for this to take effect. See also “When opening a file in a split pane, should it be a vertical split?”";
"Pasteboard: Separator inserted between multiple clippings when sending or copying them.\nThe value should use Vim syntax, such as \\n for newline and \\e for escape." = "Pasteboard: Separator inserted between multiple clippings when sending or copying them.\nThe value should use Vim syntax, such as \\n for newline and \\e for escape.";
"Pasteboard: Delay in seconds before the Return that submits an auto-sent code review.\nWhen the code-review workgroup toolbar auto-sends review results to the main session, it pastes the results and then sends a Return to submit them. This is how long it waits, after the pasted results finish, before sending that Return. A paste-debouncing TUI (such as Claude Code) processes a paste and a Return that arrive together as one batch, so the Return can be swallowed and the review never submits; the delay makes the Return land in a later read so it submits. The default matches Claude Code’s 500 ms paste-settling window. Set to 0 to send it immediately." = "Pasteboard: Delay in seconds before the Return that submits an auto-sent code review.\nWhen the code-review workgroup toolbar auto-sends review results to the main session, it pastes the results and then sends a Return to submit them. This is how long it waits, after the pasted results finish, before sending that Return. A paste-debouncing TUI (such as Claude Code) processes a paste and a Return that arrive together as one batch, so the Return can be swallowed and the review never submits; the delay makes the Return land in a later read so it submits. The default matches Claude Code’s 500 ms paste-settling window. Set to 0 to send it immediately.";
"Quad-click selects a string under the cursor that best matches a regular expression from a collection of rules." = "四击会选中光标下方与一组规则中的正则表达式最匹配的字符串。";
